  "text": "To find the point \\( E \\) such that the four small triangles \\( AEB \\), \\( AEC \\), \\( BED \\), and \\( CED \\) all have equal perimeters, we need to use the properties of the parallelogram and the fact that the diagonals bisect each other. The midpoints of the diagonals will be used as a reference point.\n\n1. **Find the midpoints of the diagonals**:\n   The diagonals of the parallelogram are \\( AC \\) and \\( BD \\). Let \\( O \\) be the centroid (midpoint) of both diagonals.\n\n   The midpoint \\( O \\) of \\( AC \\):\n   \\[\n   O = \\left( \\frac{2+8}{2}, \\frac{3+9}{2} \\right) = (5, 6)\n   \\]\n\n   The midpoint \\( O \\) of \\( BD \\):\n   \\[\n   O = \\left( \\frac{6+4}{2}, \\frac{5+7}{2} \\right) = (5, 6)\n   \\]\n\n2. **Find the coordinates of \\( E \\)**:\n   Since \\( E \\) is the point inside the parallelogram where the four triangles have equal perimeters, \\( E \\) will be at the centroid of the parallelogram. However, to verify, we can use the property that \\( E \\) is the centroid when the triangles have equal perimeters.\n\n   If we set up the perimeter equations, the centroid \\( O \\) must have the same properties. Thus we set \\( E \\) as:\n   \\[\n   E = (5, 6)\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate \\( 5x + 2y \\)**:\n   Substituting the coordinates \\( (x, y) \\) of \\( E \\):\n   \\[\n   5x + 2y = 5(5) + 2(6) = 25 + 12 = 37\n   \\]\n\nSo, the value of \\( 5x + 2y \\) is:\n\n\\boxed{37}",
